==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|fedwōr|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|kʷetwóres|lang=ine-pro}}.

Common Germanic cognates include Old High German [[fior]] (German ''[[vier]]''), Old Saxon [[fiuwar]], Old Dutch [[viuwar]] (Dutch ''[[vier]]''), Old Norse ''[[fjórir]]'', Gothic {{l|got|𐍆𐌹𐌳𐍅𐍉𐍂}}.

Indo-European cognates include Proto-Slavic {{recons|četiri|lang=sla-pro}} (Russian {{l|ru|четыре}}), Old Irish [[cethair]], Greek {{term|τέσσαρες|lang=grc}}, Latin ''[[quattuor]]''.

